\section*{Challenge 24: Rota's Basis Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Disjoint bases and rainbow bases]
Let $M$ be a matroid of rank $n$ on a finite ground set, and let $B_1,\dots,B_n$ be bases of $M$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The family $B_1,\dots,B_n$ is \emph{disjoint} if the sets $B_i$ are pairwise disjoint.
\item A family $C_1,\dots,C_n$ of bases of $M$ is a system of \emph{rainbow bases} for $B_1,\dots,B_n$ if $B_i \cap C_j$ is a singleton for all $i,j \in \{1,\dots,n\}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{conjecture}[Rota's Basis Conjecture \mbox{\cite[Conjecture~4]{huang1994relations}}]
Let $n \in \N$ and let $M$ be a matroid of rank $n$. For every family of $n$ pairwise disjoint bases $B_1,\dots,B_n$ of $M$, there exist $n$ pairwise disjoint rainbow bases $C_1,\dots,C_n$ of $M$ --- that is, with $B_i \cap C_j$ a singleton for all $i,j$.
\end{conjecture}

Rota's basis conjecture is open in general. The challenge originally posed a \emph{scaled weakening} --- asking, for a parameter $r$, only for roughly $\ev{1 - \tfrac{1}{r} - \varepsilon}n$ rainbow bases --- and this weakening is a theorem: Buci\'c, Kwan, Pokrovskiy and Sudakov established the ``halfway'' bound \cite{bucic2020halfway}. Since the scaled weakening that motivated the challenge is already resolved in the literature, the problem is recorded here in its universal form rather than as an open challenge.

