\section*{Challenge 38: Thomassen's Conjecture (4-Connected Line Graphs are Hamiltonian)}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Line graph]
Let $G = \ev{V, E}$ be a finite simple graph. The \emph{line graph} $L\ev{G}$ of $G$ is the simple graph whose vertex set is $E$, with two distinct edges $e, f \in E$ adjacent in $L\ev{G}$ if and only if they share an endvertex in $G$. A graph is called a \emph{line graph} if it is isomorphic to $L\ev{G}$ for some simple graph $G$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$k$-connectivity]
A finite simple graph $H$ on more than $k$ vertices is \emph{$k$-connected} if the removal of any set of fewer than $k$ vertices leaves a connected graph.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hamiltonian graph]
A finite simple graph $H$ is \emph{Hamiltonian} if it contains a cycle visiting every vertex exactly once. (By convention the one-vertex graph is Hamiltonian and the empty graph is not.)
\end{definition}

\begin{conjecture}[Thomassen \mbox{\cite{thomassen}}]\label{thomassen}
Every $4$-connected line graph is Hamiltonian. Equivalently: for every finite simple graph $G$, if $L\ev{G}$ is $4$-connected, then $L\ev{G}$ is Hamiltonian.
\end{conjecture}

The conjecture, posed by Thomassen in 1986, is open. It sits in a remarkable web of equivalences and partial results.

\emph{Upper bound (stronger conjecture).} Line graphs contain no induced $K_{1,3}$ (they are \emph{claw-free}), so Conjecture~\ref{thomassen} is implied by the Matthews--Sumner conjecture \cite{matthewssumner} that every $4$-connected claw-free graph is Hamiltonian. Ryj\'a\v{c}ek's closure theory \cite{ryjacek} shows the two conjectures are in fact \emph{equivalent}.

\emph{Lower bound (known cases).} Zhan \cite{zhan} (and independently Jackson) proved that every $7$-connected line graph is Hamiltonian. Kaiser and Vr\'ana \cite{kaiservrana} proved that every $5$-connected line graph of minimum degree at least $6$ is Hamiltonian, via a theorem producing spanning trees with prescribed degree behaviour; relatedly, if $G$ has two edge-disjoint spanning trees then $G$ has a spanning closed trail (Jaeger), whence $L\ev{G}$ is Hamiltonian. The case of $4$-connected line graphs remains open even for line graphs of cubic graphs.

\emph{Reformulation in the base graph.} By Harary--Nash-Williams \cite{hararynashwilliams}, for a graph $G$ with at least three edges, $L\ev{G}$ is Hamiltonian if and only if $G$ contains a \emph{dominating closed trail} (a closed trail $T$ such that every edge of $G$ has an endvertex on $T$). Moreover $L\ev{G}$ is $4$-connected if and only if $G$ is \emph{essentially $4$-edge-connected} (every edge cut of fewer than $4$ edges leaves a component with no edge). Hence Conjecture~\ref{thomassen} is equivalent to: every essentially $4$-edge-connected graph has a dominating closed trail.

\emph{The cubic case suffices.} For a connected cubic (3-regular) graph $G$, closed trails are cycles, so the reformulation becomes Fleischner's conjecture that every essentially $4$-edge-connected (equivalently, cyclically $4$-edge-connected) cubic graph has a dominating cycle. Fleischner and Jackson \cite{fleischnerjackson} proved that this cubic special case is already equivalent to the full Conjecture~\ref{thomassen}. Tom\'a\v{s} Kaiser (personal communication, 2026) believes a counterexample should exist among line graphs of cubic graphs --- i.e.\ a cyclically $4$-edge-connected cubic graph without a dominating cycle --- though none has been found; this is a natural target for automated counterexample search.

