\section*{Challenge 79: Antipodal Edge-Colourings of the Hypercube}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Hypercube, antipodes, antipodal colourings]
For $d \ge 1$ the \emph{hypercube} $Q_d$ is the graph whose vertices are the $0/1$-vectors of length $d$, two of them being adjacent when they differ in exactly one coordinate. The \emph{antipode} $\bar x$ of a vertex $x$ is obtained by complementing every coordinate; the antipode of an edge $xy$ is the edge $\bar x\bar y$. A two-colouring $c$ of the edges of $Q_d$ is \emph{antipodal} if every edge and its antipode receive opposite colours. No properness is imposed on $c$. A path is \emph{monochromatic} if all its edges have the same colour.
\end{definition}

\begin{conjecture}[Norine]\label{antipodal}
Let $d \ge 2$. For every antipodal two-colouring of the edges of $Q_d$ there is a pair of antipodal vertices joined by a monochromatic path.
\end{conjecture}

Norine \cite{norine2008} posed the conjecture on the Open Problem Garden, together with the stronger statement that \emph{every} two-colouring of the edges of $Q_d$ (antipodal or not) admits a path between two antipodal vertices with at most one change of colour; the stronger statement implies the conjecture. Feder and Subi \cite{federsubi2013} studied both statements and verified them in small dimensions. The conjecture is open in general.

In the formalisation the vertex type is $\mathrm{Fin}\ d \to \mathrm{Bool}$, the colouring is an arbitrary map on unordered pairs of vertices (only its values on edges matter), and the conclusion asks for a monochromatic walk between antipodal vertices; a monochromatic path follows by removing repeated vertices.

