\section*{Challenge 80: The Reconstruction Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Cards and decks]
For a finite simple graph $G$ and a vertex $v$, the \emph{card} of $G$ at $v$ is the vertex-deleted subgraph $G - v$, the subgraph induced on the remaining vertices. The \emph{deck} of $G$ is the multiset of isomorphism classes of its cards. Two graphs $G$ and $G'$ have the \emph{same deck} when there is a bijection $\sigma$ between their vertex sets such that $G - v$ is isomorphic to $G' - \sigma(v)$ for every vertex $v$; the isomorphisms of the individual cards are not required to agree with each other or with $\sigma$, and multiplicities are retained.
\end{definition}

\begin{conjecture}[Kelly--Ulam reconstruction conjecture]\label{reconstruction}
Two finite simple graphs on at least three vertices with the same deck are isomorphic.
\end{conjecture}

The conjecture was formulated by Kelly \cite{kelly1957} and Ulam \cite{ulam1960}; the bound $3$ is sharp, since the two graphs on two vertices have the same deck. It is known for many classes (disconnected graphs, trees, regular graphs, outerplanar graphs, \dots) and has been verified by computer for small orders; see Bondy's manual \cite{bondy1991}. Yang \cite{yang1988} showed that it suffices to reconstruct $2$-connected graphs. Kelly's lemma, that the number of subgraphs of $G$ isomorphic to a fixed graph on fewer vertices is determined by the deck, yields the number of edges and the degree sequence; the survey \cite{survey2026} discusses such invariants from the point of view of invariant theory. The conjecture is open.

