\section*{Challenge 82: Graham's Graph-Pebbling Product Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

A pebbling configuration on a finite simple graph $G$ is a function $a:V(G)\to\mathbb{N}$, of size $|a|=\sum_{v\in V(G)}a(v)$. A legal move removes two pebbles from a vertex carrying at least two pebbles and places one at an adjacent vertex, leaving all other coordinates unchanged. A configuration is $r$-solvable if a finite sequence of legal moves, possibly empty, produces a configuration with at least one pebble at $r$.

For connected $G$, let $\pi(G)$ be the least $n$ such that every configuration of size $n$ is $r$-solvable for every root $r$. Equivalently, one may require every configuration of size at least $n$. The threshold exists: $|V(G)|2^{|V(G)|}$ pebbles force a stack of at least $2^{|V(G)|}$ at some vertex, and a path of length $\ell$ needs only $2^\ell$ pebbles at its starting vertex.

\begin{conjecture}[Graham, stated in \cite{Chung1989Pebbling}]
For all finite connected simple graphs $G$ and $H$,
\[\pi(G\mathbin{\square}H)\leq\pi(G)\pi(H).\]
\end{conjecture}
The Cartesian product has vertices $(v,w)$; two vertices are adjacent precisely when one coordinate stays fixed and the other moves along an edge of its factor. Connectedness includes nonemptiness. The conjecture concerns all initial configurations and all roots, not merely an optimally chosen configuration. It remains open; see \cite{PulajWoodYerger2024Pebbling}.

