\section*{Challenge 83: Pebbling Number of a Path}
\refstepcounter{section}

Let $P_{n+1}$ be the path with vertices $0,\ldots,n$ and edges between consecutive vertices. A configuration $a$ assigns a nonnegative integer number of pebbles to each vertex. A legal move removes two pebbles from one vertex and places one at an adjacent vertex. A configuration is $r$-solvable if a finite sequence of legal moves, possibly empty, leaves a pebble at $r$. The pebbling number $\pi(G)$ is the least $N$ such that every configuration of $N$ pebbles is $r$-solvable for every vertex $r$. Equivalently, one may require at least $N$ pebbles.

\begin{theorem}
For every integer $n\geq 0$,
\[
\pi(P_{n+1})=2^n.
\]
\end{theorem}
This includes the single-vertex path, whose pebbling number is $1$. The result is classical; see \cite{Hurlbert2011PebblingWeights}, Section 2. A complete solution is supplied as a more approachable solved benchmark.

