\section*{Challenge 85: Lonely Runner Conjecture (Integer Speeds)}
\refstepcounter{section}

For $x\in\mathbb{R}$, write $\|x\|_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}$ for its distance to the nearest integer. This is the norm of $x$ in the unit circle group $\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$.

\begin{conjecture}[Lonely Runner Conjecture, integer formulation]
For every integer $k\geq1$ and nonzero integers $v_1,\ldots,v_k$, there exists $t\in\mathbb{R}$ such that
\[
\|tv_i\|_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}\geq\frac{1}{k+1}
\qquad (1\leq i\leq k).
\]
\end{conjecture}

There is one stationary runner and $k$ other speeds, so the denominator is $k+1$. All runners start at the same point. The integer formulation allows negative and repeated speeds; no independent starting positions are allowed. It is not the shifted lonely-runner conjecture. The formal statement uses mathlib's \texttt{UnitAddCircle} and its norm directly, with no bespoke distance definition. Mathlib identifies this norm with $|x-\operatorname{round}(x)|$, where the rounded integer is nearest to $x$.

The standard reduction from arbitrary real speeds to the integer formulation is discussed in \cite{PerarnauSerra2025LonelyRunner}; that reduction is not formalized here. The integer-speed conjecture is the explicit central statement. The private library does prove sign symmetry, common nonzero integer scaling, reindexing, and reduction of time modulo one.

The general conjecture remains open. Computer-assisted results for eleven through thirteen runners are reported in \cite{SungkawichaiTrakulthongchai2026LonelyRunner}. A preprint submitted on September 2, 2026 reports fourteen runners \cite{Allikvere2026Fourteen}; its certificates have not been independently checked in this task. The companion disproof file asks for a nonzero integer speed vector such that every real time violates the proposed bound for at least one speed.

