\section*{Problem 86: Rainbow trees in subgraphs of hypercubes}
Let $Q_n$ have vertex set $\{0,1\}^n$, with two vertices adjacent precisely when they differ in exactly one coordinate. A proper edge-colouring assigns different colours to any two distinct edges sharing a vertex. A rainbow copy of a graph $T$ in an edge-coloured graph $G$ is an injective edge-preserving map $f:V(T)\to V(G)$ such that distinct edges of $T$ have differently coloured images. The copy need not be induced.

\begin{theorem}[Crawford--Sankar--Schildkraut--Spiro, Theorem 1.2]
Let $G$ be a nonempty finite subgraph of a hypercube. Every proper edge-colouring of $G$ contains a rainbow copy of every finite tree $T$ with $|E(T)|\leq\delta(G)$.
\end{theorem}

The ambient dimension is independent of the size of $T$. The nonempty-host convention includes the one-vertex tree and minimum degree zero. In the formal statement, an injective graph homomorphism from $G$ to $Q_n$ represents an arbitrary subgraph, a colouring of the line graph represents proper edge-colouring, and injectivity of the pulled-back colouring expresses the rainbow condition. No requirement is imposed on images of nonedges.

