\section*{Problem 87: Galvin's bipartite list-edge-colouring theorem}
Let $M=(V,E)$ be a finite loopless multigraph; each edge object has an unordered pair of distinct endpoints, and parallel edge objects are allowed. Suppose the vertices can be partitioned into two parts such that each edge joins the two parts. Write $E(v)$ for the incident edge objects at $v$.

\begin{theorem}[Galvin]
Let $d\geq 0$ satisfy $|E(v)|\leq d$ for every vertex $v$. For any palette $C$ and finite lists $L(e)\subseteq C$ with $|L(e)|\geq d$, there is a function $c:E\to C$ satisfying $c(e)\in L(e)$ for every edge and injective on $E(v)$ for every vertex $v$.
\end{theorem}

This is the usual maximum-degree formulation with an arbitrary degree upper bound $d$. The palette need not be finite and no connectedness is assumed. Parallel edges count separately towards degrees and must receive distinct colours. The empty graph and empty-edge cases are included.

