\section*{Problem 89: Erdős--Szekeres monotone subsequences}
Let $r,s\geq 1$ and let $a_0,\ldots,a_{n-1}$ be pairwise distinct elements of a linearly ordered set.
\begin{theorem}[Erdős--Szekeres]
If $n>(r-1)(s-1)$, then there are indices
$i_0<\cdots<i_{r-1}$ with $a_{i_0}<\cdots<a_{i_{r-1}}$,
or indices $j_0<\cdots<j_{s-1}$ with $a_{j_0}>\cdots>a_{j_{s-1}}$.
\end{theorem}

A subsequence may skip positions; its indices remain strictly increasing in both alternatives. In Lean, an order embedding $\operatorname{Fin}(r)\hookrightarrow_o\operatorname{Fin}(n)$ encodes precisely such an index selection. Strict monotonicity or strict antitonicity of the composition expresses the required order of the selected values. Injectivity of the original sequence expresses distinctness. The size-one cases are included.

