\section*{Challenge 94: Explicit Lossless Vertex Expanders}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Two-sided vertex expanders]
A bipartite graph with sides $L$, $R$ is $(d_L, d_R)$-biregular if every vertex of $L$ has degree $d_L$ and every vertex of $R$ has degree $d_R$. It is a \emph{two-sided $\gamma$-vertex expander} with density threshold $\eta > 0$ if every $S \subseteq L$ with $|S| \le \eta |L|$ has $|N(S)| \ge \gamma d_L |S|$ and every $T \subseteq R$ with $|T| \le \eta |R|$ has $|N(T)| \ge \gamma d_R |T|$. A family $(Z_n)$ is a two-sided $\gamma$-vertex expander if one threshold $\eta$ works for all its members.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Hsieh--Lubotzky--Mohanty--Reiner--Zhang, Theorem 2.2 \cite{hsieh2025lossless}]\label{lossless}
For every $\varepsilon \in (0,1]$ there is $k = k(\varepsilon)$ such that for every $\beta \in (0,1]$ there is $d_0 = d_0(\varepsilon,\beta)$ with the following property. For all $d_L, d_R \ge d_0$ with $\beta \le d_L/d_R \le \beta + \varepsilon$ there is an infinite family of $(k d_L, k d_R)$-biregular bipartite graphs on $\Theta(n)$ vertices which is a two-sided $(1-\varepsilon)$-vertex expander.
\end{theorem}

The paper constructs the family in polynomial time as a tripartite line product of structured bipartite graphs coming from Ramanujan Cayley cubical complexes with a fixed gadget; the algorithmic clause is not asserted in the formalisation, which records existence with the paper's parameters. Footnote 1 of the paper turns $(1-\varepsilon)$-vertex expansion into $(1-2\varepsilon)$-unique-neighbour expansion.

